---
import BaseLayout from "../components/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About — eggx"
  description="How eggx verifies offers, what evidence means, and why free is not unlimited."
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="site-header">
      <h1>About</h1>
      <p>Editorial policy, evidence standards, and honest limits.</p>
    </header>

    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Evidence standards</h2>
      <p>
        Every offer carries an evidence level that says how confident we are that
        it currently works:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>official</strong> — current provider documentation or program
          page confirms the offer.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>community</strong> — multiple reproducible reports exist, but the
          provider's terms are incomplete or ambiguous.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>unverified</strong> — a useful lead whose official evidence has
          not yet been found.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        A third-party source can lead to an entry, but it can never be the only
        evidence. We mark provisional and community reports clearly instead of
        presenting them as confirmed.
      </p>

      <h2>Free is not unlimited</h2>
      <p>
        A free offer rarely means an unlimited one. Unknown rate limits stay
        unknown: we show them as <em>not published</em> rather than inferring
        they are unlimited. Read each offer's limits and access requirements
        before relying on it, especially for production or high-volume work.
      </p>

      <h2>Policy</h2>
      <p>
        eggx is a read-only directory. It collects no user data and stores no
        secrets. Removed or expired offers are archived rather than deleted, so the
        public audit trail is preserved. An unavailable source is shown as a prior
        verification — never as evidence of continued availability.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>